Subject: [PATCH] rpc: a client tells the server which graphs it can free

An RPC server keeps every graph it is sent, so that the client can have it
computed again by uid, and drops them only when GRAPH_CACHE_MAX (1,024) have
come or the connection closes. Each copy holds the graph and all its tensors:
about 4 MiB for a 27B model. A client sends a new graph for every batch whose
shape differs from the last one, which while a prompt is read is every
512-token batch (the padded KV size changes) and about every second 128-token
one. A PS5 worker serving Qwen3.8-27B lost 7.5 to 9 MiB of its heap per 1,000
prompt tokens read and died with "Failed to allocate input buffer" after about
175K tokens; a CPU ggml-rpc-server grows the same way, up to 1,024 graphs.

The server now sets a bit in the fourth byte of its HELLO answer (padding
before, 0 from older servers) and takes RPC_CMD_GRAPH_FREE {device, uid}. A
client that sees the bit keeps the 8 graphs it computed last on each device and
sends GRAPH_FREE for each one that leaves that list; if it computes a freed
graph again it sends it again, as for a new one. A client that does not see the
bit, and a server whose client never sends the command, behave as before, so
either side can be updated alone (the worker stops leaking once both are).
GGML_RPC_NO_GRAPH_FREE on either side turns it off.

